{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778531063872,"sessionID":"ses_1e749d986ffewTB4N5iqhFiZYg","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-bc06ed1646c61210","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json"},"output":"<path>/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\n2:   \"body\": \"Please check that the term does not already exist by using the ontology search tool OLS:\\nhttps://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl\\n\\n\\n**Preferred term label**\\nstem cell memory CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell\\n\\n**Synonyms** (add reference(s), please)\\n- Exact Synonyms:  \\n    stem cell memory CD4-positive, alpha-beta T lymphocyte\\n    stem cell memory CD4+ alpha-beta T cell\\n    stem cell memory CD4+ T-cell\\n    stem cell memory CD4+ T-lymphocyte\\n    CD4-positive TSCM cell - PMID: [21926977](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21926977/)\\n    CD4+ TSCM cell - PMID: [21926977](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21926977/)\\n    CD4+ T memory stem cell - PMID: [21926977](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21926977/)\\n    stem cell–like memory CD4+ T cell\\n    stem cell–like memory CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell\\n\\n**Definition** (free text, with reference(s), please. PubMed ID format is PMID:XXXXXX)\\nstem cell memory CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell:\\nA CD4-positive memory alpha-beta T cell with stem-like properties that is long-lived, retains a naïve-like phenotype, and exhibits self-renewal and multipotent differentiation capacity. This cell acts as a stem-like reservoir capable of regenerating central and effector memory T cell subsets.\\nReferences: PMID:19525962; PMID:21926977; PMID:28060797\\n\\n**Parent cell type term** (check the hierarchy here https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl)\\nstem cell memory CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell → [CD4-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000897)  \\n\\n**Anatomical structure where the cell type is found** (check Uberon for anatomical structures: https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/uberon)\\n\\n**Your ORCID**\\nhttps://orcid.org/0000-0001-5742-4697\\nhttps://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277\\n\\n**Additional notes or concerns**\\n\\n\\n--- \\n\\n**Preferred term label**\\nstem cell memory CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell\\n\\n**Synonyms** (add reference(s), please)\\n- Exact Synonyms:  \\n    stem cell mem... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n3:   \"comments\": [\n4:     {\n5:       \"id\": \"IC_kwDOAjLh-87ez-4i\",\n6:       \"author\": {\n7:         \"login\": \"Caroline-99\"\n8:       },\n9:       \"authorAssociation\": \"MEMBER\",\n10:       \"body\": \"This should be a seperate ticket: \\n\\nAdditional notes or concerns from [KazuhiroNakagawa](https://github.com/KazuhiroNakagawa)\\nRegarding memory T cell subsets (TCM, TEM), these terms are currently annotated with an in taxon constraint for human, but their labels do not include \\\", human\\\".\\nIs there a clear rule or convention within the Cell Ontology for labeling species-specific cell types?\\nFor example:\\n\\ncentral memory CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell (or CD8) [TCM]\\neffector memory CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell (or CD8) [TEM]\\nIn addition, the following marker sets are commonly used for stem cell memory T cells (TSCM):\\n\\nHuman: CD45RA⁺ CCR7⁺ CD62L⁺ CD95⁺\\nMouse: CD62L^high CD44^low Sca-1⁺ CD122⁺ CD27⁺ CD127⁺\\nSince the marker expression profiles of TSCM differ between humans and mice,\\nit would be preferable to establish separate terms for each species.\\nIn that case, creating corresponding mouse terms for TCM, TEM would help maintain consistency across the entire memory T cell subset structure.\\n\\nTo clearly distinguish naïve T cells from TSCM, the expression of CD95 (Fas) is an important marker in humans. However, the current naïve T cell term is shared between humans and mice.\\nTo accurately represent CD95 expression differences, it may be preferable to create separate naïve T cell terms for humans and mice.\\n\\nnaive thymus-derived CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell (or CD8)\\nHuman: CD95⁻ (CD45RA⁺ CCR7⁺ CD62L⁺)\\nMouse: CD95 not included in the definition.\",\n11:       \"createdAt\": \"2026-01-12T11:46:35Z\",\n12:       \"includesCreatedEdit\": false,\n13:       \"isMinimized\": false,\n14:       \"minimizedReason\": \"\",\n15:       \"reactionGroups\": [],\n16:       \"url\": \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3452#issuecomment-3738168866\",\n17:       \"viewerDidAuthor\": false\n18:     }\n19:   ],\n20:   \"title\": \"[NTR] Add new terms for stem cell memory T cells (TSCM): CD4+ and CD8+ subsets\",\n21:   \"url\": \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3452\"\n22: }\n\n(End of file - total 22 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\n  \"body\": \"Please check that the term does not already exist by using the ontology search tool OLS:\\nhttps://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl\\n\\n\\n**Preferred term label**\\nstem cell memory CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell\\n\\n**Synonyms** (add reference(s), please)\\n- Exact Synonyms:  \\n    stem cell memory CD4-positive, alpha-beta T lymphocyte\\n    stem cell memory CD4+ alpha-beta T cell\\n    stem cell memory CD4+ T-cell\\n    stem cell memory CD4+ T-lymphocyte\\n    CD4-positive TSCM cell - PMID: [21926977](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21926977/)\\n    CD4+ TSCM cell - PMID: [21926977](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21926977/)\\n    CD4+ T memory stem cell - PMID: [21926977](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21926977/)\\n    stem cell–like memory CD4+ T cell\\n    stem cell–like memory CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell\\n\\n**Definition** (free text, with reference(s), please. PubMed ID format is PMID:XXXXXX)\\nstem cell memory CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell:\\nA CD4-positive memory alpha-beta T cell with stem-like properties that is long-lived, retains a naïve-like phenotype, and exhibits self-renewal and multipotent differentiation capacity. This cell acts as a stem-like reservoir capable of regenerating central and effector memory T cell subsets.\\nReferences: PMID:19525962; PMID:21926977; PMID:28060797\\n\\n**Parent cell type term** (check the hierarchy here https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl)\\nstem cell memory CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell → [CD4-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000897)  \\n\\n**Anatomical structure where the cell type is found** (check Uberon for anatomical structures: https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/uberon)\\n\\n**Your ORCID**\\nhttps://orcid.org/0000-0001-5742-4697\\nhttps://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277\\n\\n**Additional notes or concerns**\\n\\n\\n--- \\n\\n**Preferred term label**\\nstem cell memory CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell\\n\\n**Synonyms** (add reference(s), please)\\n- Exact Synonyms:  \\n    stem cell mem... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n  \"comments\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"IC_kwDOAjLh-87ez-4i\",\n      \"author\": {\n        \"login\": \"Caroline-99\"\n      },\n      \"authorAssociation\": \"MEMBER\",\n      \"body\": \"This should be a seperate ticket: \\n\\nAdditional notes or concerns from [KazuhiroNakagawa](https://github.com/KazuhiroNakagawa)\\nRegarding memory T cell subsets (TCM, TEM), these terms are currently annotated with an in taxon constraint for human, but their labels do not include \\\", human\\\".\\nIs there a clear rule or convention within the Cell Ontology for labeling species-specific cell types?\\nFor example:\\n\\ncentral memory CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell (or CD8) [TCM]\\neffector memory CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell (or CD8) [TEM]\\nIn addition, the following marker sets are commonly used for stem cell memory T cells (TSCM):\\n\\nHuman: CD45RA⁺ CCR7⁺ CD62L⁺ CD95⁺\\nMouse: CD62L^high CD44^low Sca-1⁺ CD122⁺ CD27⁺ CD127⁺\\nSince the marker expression profiles of TSCM differ between humans and mice,\\nit would be preferable to establish separate terms for each species.\\nIn that case, creating corresponding mouse terms for TCM, TEM would help maintain consistency across the entire memory T cell subset structure.\\n\\nTo clearly distinguish naïve T cells from TSCM, the expression of CD95 (Fas) is an important marker in humans. However, the current naïve T cell term is shared between humans and mice.\\nTo accurately represent CD95 expression differences, it may be preferable to create separate naïve T cell terms for humans and mice.\\n\\nnaive thymus-derived CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell (or CD8)\\nHuman: CD95⁻ (CD45RA⁺ CCR7⁺ CD62L⁺)\\nMouse: CD95 not included in the definition.\",\n      \"createdAt\": \"2026-01-12T11:46:35Z\",\n      \"includesCreatedEdit\": false,\n      \"isMinimized\": false,\n      \"minimizedReason\": \"\",\n      \"reactionGroups\": [],\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3452#issuecomment-3738168866\",\n      \"viewerDidAuthor\": false\n    }\n  ],\n  \"title\": \"[NTR] Add new terms for stem cell memory T cells (TSCM): CD4+ and CD8+ subsets\",","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"__issue_context__.json","time":{"start":1778531063855,"end":1778531063870}},"id":"prt_e18b64bdb001Sq6YPbft4P9CSh","sessionID":"ses_1e749d986ffewTB4N5iqhFiZYg","messageID":"msg_e18b6273d001ZYlwqhNsyRhhHE"}}
